WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:08.211
Picture your program as a page of code, and something that walks through it, one line at a time.

2
00:00:08.433 --> 00:00:13.944
It keeps its notes on a stack: one frame per call in progress, with its local variables.

3
00:00:14.167 --> 00:00:17.144
Step into a call, and a frame goes on top.

4
00:00:17.144 --> 00:00:18.916
Return, and it comes off.

5
00:00:19.133 --> 00:00:20.884
This walker is a thread.

6
00:00:20.884 --> 00:00:23.584
The JVM starts this one, called main.

7
00:00:24.200 --> 00:00:29.476
It runs inside a process, one running JVM, with memory of its own.

8
00:00:29.700 --> 00:00:33.760
Every object you create with new lives in one part of it: the heap.

9
00:00:33.967 --> 00:00:38.138
A second thread, a worker, walks the same code with its own stack.

10
00:00:38.138 --> 00:00:41.297
Both call settle, and each one gets its own frame.

11
00:00:41.500 --> 00:00:44.406
But both can reach the same Payment on the heap.

12
00:00:44.406 --> 00:00:51.007
That is how threads share data with no copying, and how two of them can change one object at the same moment.

13
00:00:51.533 --> 00:00:53.871
Main sets its local total to three.

14
00:00:53.871 --> 00:00:55.941
Does the worker's total change?

15
00:00:57.567 --> 00:00:57.782
No.

16
00:00:57.782 --> 00:01:00.660
A local lives on its own thread's stack.

17
00:01:00.660 --> 00:01:02.387
Only the heap is shared.

18
00:01:03.300 --> 00:01:06.997
A core of the CPU runs a thread, one thread at a time.

19
00:01:07.200 --> 00:01:14.685
On one core, the operating system lets main run for a short slice, saves its registers, and switches to the worker.

20
00:01:14.685 --> 00:01:18.200
That is a context switch, thousands of times a second.

21
00:01:18.433 --> 00:01:20.953
Both are in progress, taking turns.

22
00:01:20.953 --> 00:01:22.393
That is concurrency.

23
00:01:22.600 --> 00:01:25.274
Two cores run both at the same instant.

24
00:01:25.274 --> 00:01:26.645
That is parallelism.

25
00:01:27.467 --> 00:01:32.039
So on four cores, is a busy job faster on eight threads than on four?

26
00:01:33.567 --> 00:01:33.824
No.

27
00:01:33.824 --> 00:01:40.418
In the lesson's demo, four threads took 121 milliseconds, and eight took 125.

28
00:01:40.418 --> 00:01:45.128
Once every core is busy, extra threads only take turns.

29
00:01:45.933 --> 00:01:48.968
Calling run on a thread is a plain method call.

30
00:01:48.968 --> 00:01:52.197
Main steps into the task itself, on its own stack.

31
00:01:52.400 --> 00:01:56.887
No new thread exists, nothing fails, and the job is just as slow.

32
00:01:57.100 --> 00:02:02.653
With start, the JVM asks the operating system for a new thread, with a new stack.

33
00:02:02.867 --> 00:02:05.945
That thread calls run, and main goes on at once.

34
00:02:06.867 --> 00:02:07.436
The rule.

35
00:02:07.436 --> 00:02:13.195
Every thread has its own stack, they all share the heap, and only start makes a new thread.

36
00:02:13.400 --> 00:02:14.413
Check yourself.

37
00:02:14.413 --> 00:02:19.477
You split a report into four slices, and call run on a new thread for each.

38
00:02:19.477 --> 00:02:21.975
On four cores, how much faster is it?

39
00:02:24.700 --> 00:02:25.570
Not faster.

40
00:02:25.570 --> 00:02:29.684
Main runs the four slices itself, one after another.
